WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.090
One of the things that's been helpful with AI

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.290
is finding documentation or

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.490
explanations of code for Bow

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.230
Torch, which is very underdeveloped and doesn't

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.810
have a lot of documentation for it.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.470
And so it does a really good job of finding explanations

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.850
for what functions do or how to organize,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.830
like how to call functions and use data because

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.370
it's hard to find good examples of how

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.070
I can use them in my own code.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:33.810
For us, I think,

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.970
for the powder doser, it's been, um, clot has

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.890
been controlling the whole thing, writing the

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.270
code that does the tests and running the tests.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.710
And it's been really nice in a lot of ways, particularly

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.510
that it can also record the data.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.750
So it runs the test and it records all the data

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.930
in a really good way. And it's the primary one

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.650
looking back at the data, so it knows where everything

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:52.970
is.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.810
And they can automatically draw conclusions on

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.310
the data, and like the data analysis part of

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.470
it as we're, um,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:00.550
like,

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:02.950
concurrently running tests, it's been really

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.110
nice. The scary part of that is that I,

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.490
even if I think I could probably figure out somewhat

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.190
quickly how to run the powder dust to myself

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:11.810
and write the code,

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.710
I have not done that, and I don't currently know.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.850
Like, if you asked me to do it myself, I couldn't

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.170
do it right now. It would take me a couple of

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:18.610
days, probably.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:19.750
Um,

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.910
yeah, that's scary.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.910
We were talking about the idea that the human

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.910
element is important in research, especially

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.110
when you're putting your name on it.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.050
that if I'm doing research,

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:33.730
like,

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.970
if Claude is doing research, then anybody can

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:36.550
push the button,

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.290
and then Claude is doing research.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.690
If I'm doing research, it should have aspects

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.330
of me in it, it should have that human element,

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.070
that it's specific to me, and

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.170
my ideas, and my work.
